assertion  XSH.ilogb.001
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       For x equal to R^k times a multiplier of 1 or 1.5 (negated or not),
           where R is FLT_RADIX, ilogb(x) is k: k is 0, 1, 3, 3, -2, -7, 5 and -3
           (multipliers below one are only used where they are exact powers of
           R).
test       t/001-values.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.002
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      disputed
since      1992
says       ilogb(0.375) is -2 (it is equivalent to casting logb(0.375) to int).
note       disputed (Q-103): see the same question on logb(): the exponent of
           0.375 is -2 by the inequality given for logb and -1 if "integral part
           of the logarithm" truncates toward zero. The test follows the
           inequality.
test       t/002-below-one.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.003
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       ilogb(MIN/R), with MIN the smallest normal value of the type and R the
           radix, is the exponent MIN_EXP minus 2 when subnormal numbers exist.
test       t/003-subnormal.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.004
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       ilogb(+0) and ilogb(-0) return FP_ILOGB0.
test       t/004-zero.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.005
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       ilogb(+Inf) and ilogb(-Inf) return INT_MAX.
test       t/005-infinity.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.006
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      1992
says       ilogb(NaN) returns FP_ILOGBNAN.
test       t/006-nan.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.007
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1992
says       ilogb(+0), (-0), (+Inf), (-Inf) and (NaN) are domain errors, reported
           through errno (EDOM) and the invalid exception as math_errhandling
           selects.
note       The tag on this passage is the IEC 60559 Floating-Point option; the
           test is NOTINUSE unless the C implementation claims it (Annex F of the
           C standard). The page also requires this on XSI systems; only the IEC
           60559 side is selected here.
test       t/007-zero-inf-nan-domain.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.008
anchor     basedefs/math.h.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1992
says       FP_ILOGB0 is INT_MIN or -INT_MAX, and FP_ILOGBNAN is INT_MAX or
           INT_MIN, both integer constant expressions.
test       t/008-macro-values.c

assertion  XSH.ilogb.009
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/ilogb.html#RETURN VALUE
anchor     basedefs/V1_chap04.html#4.23
class      required
since      1992
says       With errno holding EDOM before the call, ilogb(8) leaves it unchanged
           and, when exception flags are in use, raises none of the invalid,
           divide-by-zero and overflow exceptions.
test       t/009-no-error.c
